NAME
    run - load and run a program

SYNOPSIS
    run path [args]

DESCRIPTION
    Loads the file to its load address (as load does) and calls its
    exec address, with the rest of the line in ARGBUF ($0F40) as the
    program's argument tail (argstr() in C). The program returns to the
    shell with RET (main returns); the files it left open are closed
    then, and a file it was writing is registered, not lost. run is a
    shell built-in.

    A command word that is not run itself is looked up in this order:
    exit; /bin/name; the other built-ins; name in the current
    directory; else "what?". The word is lower-cased first, so any case
    finds it (names on the disk are lower case since 2026-11-03). So a /bin program wins over a built-in of the same
    name, and `hello x` is short for `run /bin/hello x`. run itself
    takes any path, exactly as typed.

LIMITS
    The program must load inside $5000..$CFFF ("bad load address or
    size"). The argument tail is 127 characters; the whole command line
    128.

EXAMPLES
    run /bin/hello a b       hello from /bin/hello, args: a b
    run /t2/hello 1 2 3      a program outside /bin
    hello a b                the same as the first line

NOTES
    Removed from the P8X version: the PATH search (Y1/OS looks in /bin
    and the current directory only) and the .bin suffix.
    The command is the Y1/OS shell's own (os/y1os.asm; os/y1os.c); the page is
    ported from P8X os/man/run 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    load, save, echo, abi
